.TH CHTTPCLIENT_ASYNC_RESULT_GET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_async_result_get \- block until the future of an async request is fulfilled, then give its typed result
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttpcli_async_result_t *chttpclient_async_result_get(ctpool_future *f);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This is a thin wrapper over ctpool_future_get. It casts the void*
result of that function to chttpcli_async_result_t*. More than one
call on the same future is safe, and this matches the contract of
ctpool_future_get. Every call after the first gives the same result
pointer. The future still owns that pointer until somebody frees it.

The result has two public fields:
  - rv holds the code that chttpclient_do gives for the same request.
  - resp is not NULL only when rv == ccol_success. The caller then owns
    resp. Free resp with chttpclient_resp_free before
    chttpclient_async_result_free.
For a request from chttpclient_do_async_streaming, a success also fills
resp. status_code therefore has the same meaning. But resp->body is NULL,
resp->body_len is 0 and resp->headers is NULL. The reason is that the
write callback got the body, and the client does not keep the headers.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
f The future from chttpclient_do_async or from
  chttpclient_do_async_streaming.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The result, or NULL. The function gives NULL if f is NULL. It also
gives NULL if something cancelled the future before it was
fulfilled.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_do_async (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_async_streaming (3),
.BR chttpclient_async_result_free (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3)
